rsz: order the setup sweep by measured yield, not by slack

Policy candidate from the repair-knobs campaign (ideas/repair-knobs-nihl.md,
third study). Not upstreamed; carried while it is measured.

The worst-first sweep spends its passes in slack order, and what an
endpoint pays has little to do with its slack. On mock-alu the 126 worst
endpoints took 80% of the cts passes for 7% of the TNS gain, and the gain
sat in endpoints reached a thousand passes later, each closing in one or
two passes; every stopping rule of the second study cut before them. The
literature never orders work by slack: TILOS picks by sensitivity, the
Lagrangian sizers by multipliers, Held by a cheap global pass first, and
all of them stop on the convergence of a return that is monotone.

So the sweep is ordered by what an endpoint has just paid. The worst
endpoint is repaired first with its full legacy budget, as before. Every
other violating endpoint gets a two-pass probe, worst first; the TNS the
probe bought per pass is its yield; an endpoint still improving when its
probe ran out goes into a heap by yield, and the heap drains best first
in two-pass rounds, an endpoint going back with its new yield while it
keeps paying. The phase ends when the heap is empty. The legacy fix-rate
exit stays; when it fires during the probes it ends the probing and the
heap still drains.

Chesterton's Fence: the worst-first order was there so that WNS gets the
first and largest budget; it keeps it. The per-endpoint patience of fifty
non-improving passes was there for endpoints that pay late; the probe
finds those in one or two passes on every design traced (0078), and an
endpoint that stops paying is dropped instead of being ground.

The repair_tns end percent is honoured as a cap on the probes and is not
expected to bind; whether it can retire is what the study measures.
